# Cloudflare Pages headers file.
#
# Takes effect only when Cloudflare Pages serves the site. GitHub Pages could
# not read this file at all, which is why it sat inert until the migration.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  # Report-only on purpose. A Content-Security-Policy that is wrong takes the
  # site down silently — scripts stop running, nobody sees an error page. In
  # report-only mode the browser enforces nothing and logs violations to its
  # console instead, so the policy can be proven against the real deployment
  # before it is allowed to block anything. Flip the header name to
  # Content-Security-Policy once a pass through the pages shows a clean console.
  #
  # Every origin below was measured against the assembled publish directory,
  # not assumed:
  #   fonts.googleapis.com     stylesheet
  #   fonts.gstatic.com        font files
  #   cdn.jsdelivr.net         gsap, ScrollTrigger, lenis, chart.js, jspdf
  #   unpkg.com                react, react-dom, @babel/standalone
  #   static.cloudflareinsights.com  analytics beacon
  #   formspree.io             form submissions
  #   calendly.com             booking embed
  #
  # 'unsafe-inline' is required and worth being honest about: the pages carry
  # inline <style> blocks and 68 inline event handlers (53 onclick, 7 each of
  # onmouseover and onmouseout, 1 onload). So this policy does not defend
  # against injected inline script. What it does defend against is an attacker
  # pointing the page at an origin that is not on this list — which is the
  # realistic failure for a static site with third-party dependencies.
  #
  # 'unsafe-eval' is gone. It lets a string become running code, and it was
  # never needed. The August version of this comment claimed the tool pages
  # required it because they compiled JSX in the browser with
  # @babel/standalone. That was an assumption, not a measurement, and it was
  # wrong: Babel inserts its output as inline <script> elements, which
  # 'unsafe-inline' covers. Tested on 26 September with Babel actually running
  # under a policy without 'unsafe-eval' — no violation — and the same harness
  # does report one for a string passed to setTimeout, so it can see eval when
  # eval happens. All eight third-party scripts on the site were also read for
  # eval(), new Function() and Function(): React, ReactDOM, jsPDF, Chart.js,
  # Lenis, GSAP, ScrollTrigger and the Cloudflare beacon. None uses them.
  # (The first draft of this list left out Chart.js. scripts/csp-report.mjs
  # caught it, by refusing to call a page clean when a library on it had not
  # run.)
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://unpkg.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self' https://formspree.io https://cloudflareinsights.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; frame-src https://calendly.com; form-action 'self' https://formspree.io; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'self'
